Host request-byte goldens
========================

These fixtures pin API-to-driver request construction, not rendered pixels or
hardware acceptance. No demo code is built or linked. Every case starts a fresh
process, uses memfds duplicated to fd 100/101, and must reach the fake /dev/rga.
The client refuses to call librga if the preload marker is absent. The runner
requires a successful intercepted open and exactly DRIVER_VERSION (return 1),
HW_VERSION (return 1), BLIT_SYNC (return 0), in that order, for every case.

G1  NV16 3840x2160 -> NV12 3840x2160, IM_SYNC, strides equal dimensions.
G2  NV12 64x64 copy.
G3  BGR 1920x1080 crop {8,8,1280,720} -> NV12 1280x720.
G4  BGR 1920x1080 -> NV12 1280x720 scale.
G5  NV12 1280x720 -> NV12 720x1280, 90-degree rotation. Both swapped widths
    satisfy the driver's 16-pixel stride alignment; no hidden padded stride.
G6  Scheduler RGA3_CORE1 and priority 3, then G1.
G7  Legacy c_RkRgaInit/rga_set_rect/c_RkRgaBlit NV16 -> NV12 at 3840x2160.
G8  RGB 1920x1080 -> NV12 1280x720, imsetColorSpace explicitly selecting
    IM_RGB_FULL -> IM_YUV_BT709_LIMIT_RANGE; the full CSC bytes are retained.

G1.bin through G8.bin are plain hex dumps (od -An -v -tx1), not binary blobs,
despite the required .bin extension. Decode with xxd -r -p G1.bin if needed.
Each represents exactly sizeof(struct rga_req) raw bytes: 504 on the initial
64-bit little-endian host baseline. No padding, field, address, or reserved byte
is masked. The comparator names the case, zero-based byte offset, and source
rectangle region on a mismatch. G1 additionally runs twice and cmp compares the
raw dumps across independent processes. Fixtures are ABI/endian-specific.

The dedicated, non-installed rga-golden-host library is compiled from the same
librga_srcs with -ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero. This is REQUIRED: upstream
NormalRgaApi.cpp:825 creates an uninitialized full_csc_t local and line 913
copies its padding into the request. Uninstrumented G8 changed at byte 318
between processes. Compiler initialization makes that padding deterministic
before request construction, without rewriting/masking captured output or
patching production source. These are instrumented-host construction goldens,
not a claim that the unmodified production library initializes all padding.
R0/R1 source comparisons must use the same instrumentation. A separate
uninstrumented test is needed to diagnose uninitialized-memory regressions.

Build and run
-------------

The parallel-todo contract forbids editing the shared root meson.build here.
The coordinator appends tests/meson-fragments/goldens.build verbatim to its end.
Until that wiring lands, make a disposable repo-local scratch copy:

    mkdir -p test-results/scratch
    git archive HEAD | tar -x -C test-results/scratch
    cp -R tests test-results/scratch/
    cat tests/meson-fragments/goldens.build >> test-results/scratch/meson.build
    meson setup test-results/scratch/build test-results/scratch -Dcpp_args=-fpermissive
    meson test -C test-results/scratch/build goldens --verbose
    meson test -C test-results/scratch/build shim-contract --verbose

Use a new/empty scratch directory, not one with already-appended wiring.
The temporary -fpermissive option is needed for the imported upstream's known
pointer-to-unsigned-int casts on 64-bit hosts. It changes no source; removing it
belongs to the later truncation fix. The new C targets use -Wall -Wextra -Werror.
The fragment exports fake_rga (the shared-object target) and fake_rga_env (an
environment with LD_PRELOAD set), reusable by the unit-session fragment.
Dependencies: Linux/glibc, GCC, Meson/Ninja, sh, coreutils, awk and grep.
Keep build/test artifacts in a gitignored repo-local directory.

Regeneration
------------

    root=$PWD
    build=$root/test-results/scratch/build
    for case in G1 G2 G3 G4 G5 G6 G7 G8; do
        sh tests/golden/run.sh --regen "$build/golden-cases" \
            "$build/libfake_rga.so" "$root/tests/golden" "$case" || break
    done

--regen is for the initial baseline and, thereafter, ONLY a commit whose message
names the fix that changed the request bytes. Never regenerate to make an
unexplained failure green. Normal Meson tests only compare, never rewrite.
Copy updated fixtures into the scratch copy before testing it. To exercise the
negative control, edit ONLY scratch/tests/golden/cases.c: change G3's crop x
from 8 to 9, run meson test ... goldens --verbose, require a G3 source-rect
byte mismatch, then restore cases.c from the original and rebuild.

Shim environment contract
-------------------------

LD_PRELOAD=/absolute/path/libfake_rga.so enables interposition. open/openat and
their glibc open64/openat64 aliases fake ONLY the literal path /dev/rga with a
memfd; all other paths, modes and directory fds are forwarded through RTLD_NEXT.
close removes fake-fd tracking. Non-fake ioctls go to RTLD_NEXT. Unknown ioctls
on a fake fd return -1/ENOTTY, never reach hardware. Capacity is 256 concurrently
open fake device fds. This host shim is not intended for 32-bit large-file ABIs,
device-fd duplication, or fork-shared driver state.

FAKE_RGA_DUMP=<path>
    Append binary INPUT bytes before output mutations or fault injection:
    BLIT_SYNC/ASYNC: one rga_req; REQUEST_SUBMIT/CONFIG: one rga_user_request
    followed by task_num rga_req structs from task_ptr. No headers or separators.
    Queries, import/release, create/cancel are logged, not dumped. The caller
    owns truncation and supplies valid argument pointers. Request task counts
    above RGA_TASK_NUM_MAX fail EINVAL. Pointer bytes in rga_user_request are
    retained, so batching clients need fixed-address task storage for cross-
    process goldens; the eight single-blit cases contain no task_ptr.

FAKE_RGA_LOG=<path>
    Append the canary log; default is ./interposed.log. Every literal device
    open attempt and every handled ioctl records fd and return/errno. The
    golden runner uses one isolated interposed.log per process and asserts
    the entire expected ioctl sequence (including return codes).

FAKE_RGA_RET_<IOCTL>=<integer>
    Override the return for the exact full symbolic ioctl name, e.g.
    FAKE_RGA_RET_RGA_BLIT_SYNC=1. Numbers use strtol base 0 (decimal/hex).
    Nonnegative overrides still populate normal output arguments; negative
    overrides skip output writes and set errno as below. Controls are read
    on every ioctl, so setenv/unsetenv fault changes work within a process.

FAKE_RGA_FAIL=<IOCTL>
    Exact full symbolic name, e.g. RGA_IOC_GET_DRVIER_VERSION (the UAPI's
    DRVIER spelling is intentional). Matching calls return -1, take precedence
    over RET overrides, and skip output writes. Persistent, not one-shot.

FAKE_RGA_ERRNO=<integer>
    errno for FAIL or a negative RET override; default EIO (5). Use numeric
    values, e.g. 22 for EINVAL. Persistent EINTR/512 injection deliberately
    makes librga retry indefinitely: use a timeout for such reproducers.

FAKE_RGA_OUT_FENCE=<integer>
    Successful BLIT_SYNC/ASYNC writes out_fence_fd; REQUEST_SUBMIT/CONFIG writes
    release_fence_fd. Default -1; 0 and positive values are copied verbatim.
    The shim does not allocate, duplicate or close this fd: a caller that tests
    ownership must supply a real fd. Failed ioctls leave output fields untouched.

FAKE_RGA_SYNC_FAIL=1
    Inject -1/EIO at poll for exactly one nonnegative fd, events=POLLIN and
    timeout=-1 (the imsync -> rga_sync_wait shape), without closing the fd.
    Logged as `poll sync-wait`. Other poll calls, and all polls when unset or
    not equal to 1, forward to RTLD_NEXT. This opt-in affects any matching poll,
    not just RGA descriptors; use it only in an isolated host test process.

FAKE_RGA_GETENV_COUNT=<path>
    At normal process exit, overwrite with a decimal count plus newline of
    external getenv calls observed by this shim. Atomic process-wide counter;
    internal FAKE_RGA_* lookups bypass it. No per-call file I/O is added to the
    perf path. _exit, crashes and killed processes do not flush the count.

Unset DUMP/GETENV_COUNT disables that output. Output-open/write errors exit 125
instead of silently losing evidence. Parent directories must already exist.
Use separate paths per process; dump/log are append-only, count is overwritten.
The runner does not clear externally supplied fault controls: an injected
failure must fail the test, not be hidden by its environment setup.

Default ioctl results and output arguments
-----------------------------------------

RGA_IOC_GET_DRVIER_VERSION: 1; version 1.3.11 (revision decimal 11).
RGA_IOC_GET_HW_VERSION: 1; cores 3.0.76831 twice, then 3.2.63318. Hardware
    revision numbers are hexadecimal, as in librga's hardware matching table.
RGA2_GET_VERSION / RGA_GET_VERSION: 1; string 3.2.63318.
RGA_BLIT_SYNC / RGA_BLIT_ASYNC: 0; out_fence_fd = -1.
RGA_IOC_IMPORT_BUFFER: 0; each external buffer gets a handle starting at 1,
    increasing process-wide. RELEASE_BUFFER: 0.
RGA_IOC_REQUEST_CREATE: 0; replaces the flags/id argument with an increasing
    process-wide id starting at 1, independently of imported-buffer handles.
RGA_IOC_REQUEST_SUBMIT / CONFIG: 0; release_fence_fd = -1.
RGA_IOC_REQUEST_CANCEL, RGA_FLUSH, RGA_GET_RESULT: 0.

The shim models successful submission, not scheduling or sync_file fences:
no actual image work, DMA import, completion fence, or request-lifetime
validation is performed. Legacy version queries are available for fault-path
tests; blit dumps model the multi-RGA rga_req ABI used by the 1.3.11 island,
including G7 (legacy userspace API, NOT the obsolete rga2_req driver ABI).
Fence lifetime/error-path reproduction is separate from byte goldens:
`bash tests/repro/run-h6.sh` runs C2/C3/C4 for 200 iterations each against the
uninstrumented shared library, recording test-results/h6/cases.csv and the
per-iteration fd census. Exit 1 means a defect was reproduced, not a build
failure; exit 2 means a harness/precondition failure. H6a positive-success
polarity is WITHDRAWN, not a test: no real positive-success submit path exists
on the island. See docs/fix-audit.d/h6.md for the observations and limits.
